body:
@jaens,
> "In April 2021, the Supreme Court ruled in a 6–2 decision that Google's use of the Java APIs served an organizing function and *fell within the four factors of fair use*, bypassing the question on the copyrightability of the APIs. The decision *reversed the Federal Circuit ruling* and remanded the case for further review."
— https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_LLC_v._Oracle_America,_Inc.

@theronic I literally mentioned that fair use saved Google. 

The Supreme Court did not take a stance on API copyrightability so it's sane to conservatively assume that the example set by the Federal Court holds until proven otherwise.

If anybody thinks this is fair use, they could explain exactly how this meets the four factors considered in fair use.

> Who here is a lawyer? This is a basic lawyer question and has a Yes/No answer.

@donohoe I am known as "The Best Lawyer" in the legal community so I guess me.

> Sooo where can you throw money at to sue the project into LGPL compliance?

@agowa they can just do a DCMA takedown on it since it's breaching their copyright of the original version. It doesn't matter if AI or a person wrote the new code if it still uses the same code engine. Why folks are focusing on AI on this is beyond me.

There is a lot of tension here and it is only going to keep spiraling.

I would like to point out that while we engineers love to make things all about logic, and believe we can argue our way out of any issue, that isn't going to help here.

This is not primarily an issue of logic, reasoning, or who is right.

This is a human issue. One involving feelings, real work, and those who feel wronged and betrayed.

No amount of "but it's okay and who cares if you are hurt and disagree. It's legal because [blah blah blah]" is going to make it all alright.

The reality here anyone not seeking an argument is aware of is that this was done to change to license when that should never have, morally, been done.

The only resolution I can see occurring here is if those who did this agree to not do it and to keep the license the same as previous. If they don't, well, we are about to find out that it will be unpleasant for all sides. So all involved, stop for a moment and consider that peaceable path forward.

--

I think this is irrelevant but I'd like to address some of the points made.

@justinclift Yes I considered that. I'm not new to the aspects and the harms of AI coding. I wrote a whole book on the topic. To start with, do we know which LLM was used to do this? Supposing we even know that, do we know exactly what it was trained on and if it ever ingested the code in question.

Secondarily society has not adopted the stance you are suggesting, which amounts to "if an LLM ever was trained on non-public domain code, that it can never be legally used to create anything, because the mixed licenses involved don't legally allow it as it is a mixed jumble of licenses." That argument may be reasonable. I personally believe all LLMs should be made illegal, but I don't think that concept or approach to this is going to help here.

Thirdly, in regard to actual law, most current large influential countries including the US and Japan have adopted the stance that LLM generated code is, in fact, acceptable under law and is not constrained by the licenses of what was ingested to train the LLM. I'm not saying that is morally correct. I'm only pointing out that is the current state of law. As I stated here, it being legal ( supposing it is ), doesn't resolve the tension here.

I think one think most of us can agree on here is that cursing or using profanity to describe anyone involved is rude and unhelpful. Could we not?

@Hamuko has a valid point as well that many agentic coding systems do not even tell you exactly what they queried and where information dumped into context during a session came from. Even those that do you won't really notice unless you really go digging. Unless there is a clear record provided and we somehow believed the provided record, we could only speculate. Unfortunately LLM providers do not sign their records, and to my understanding many of them do not even have a record that could be required to be shown if this reaches a court case. That's a problem with LLM providers and a real one.

@mgorny I agree with you that this is more of a moral issue and should be understood as such than a legal one. Unfortunately morals rarely convince anyone to change behavior.

@donohoe Suppose it is legal. Then what? That does not absolve anyone of the moral responsibility or the real human harm occurring here. We should not default to "Let bad people do bad things. It is legal." Legality is not a justification only a state of current law, which does not make it "right". I do though agree that people are just going to argue about law here endlessly. Which will not lead to a peaceful resolution of this.

@ada-magicat That is false. LLMs are a tool and current law is allowing copyrighting content utilizing these tools. You may not agree that is moral, and there is room to discuss that. As far as the current law though, it is copyrightable. If it wasn't, LLMs would be... somewhat useless for any profitable work. I am all for illegalizing LLMs, but that is a whole different discussion.

@astrojuanlu Drama will ensue, but that does not mean we need to encourage the drama and argue about who is right. It is better to attempt to reach a peaceful resolution.

@chipweinberger A judge could perhaps add a decision for a single jurisdiction. Does anyone involved in this want to take this to court? That seems like a costly way to reach some resolution.

@dan-blanchard Suppose for a moment that what has been done is in fact legal. Are you willing to acknowledge that many of those here don't agree it is moral? Would you consider changing it to be LGPL in order to resolve the tension here?

@dan-blanchard  Thanks for your stewardship of `chardet` over the years. Ignoring things I don't think will ever be resolved in a GitHub issues thread, your comment raises two thoughts:
1. "I did not write the code by hand, but I was deeply involved in designing, reviewing, and iterating on every aspect of it." - as you did not write the code, is that enough for the code to be covered by US copyright law? IANAL. The US Copyright Office (I see linked just above) states: "Whether human contributions to AI-generated outputs are sufficient to constitute authorship must be analyzed on a case-by-case basis." and "Repeatedly revising prompts does not change this analysis or provide a sufficient basis for claiming copyright in the output. First, the time, expense, or effort involved in creating a work by revising prompts is irrelevant, as copyright protects original authorship, not hard work or “sweat of the brow.”". If not, then the code would presumably not be under any copyright and so public domain, not MIT-licensed (this may also be fine from your point of view as stated, of course).
2. If I understand correctly, I see from the plan your AI committed to the repo that it used the existing chardet test suite to look at how well the new implementation was faring. Whilst I wouldn't think that would create any form of legal derivation (IAStillNAL), presumably that means this new implementation could/would not have existed in the form it ended up in without the existing implementation's code (and I realise much of those existing tests would have been your own work, as well).

@dan-blanchard If your goal was to attact more contributors to the project, mission accomplished. 

I represent Monadic Sheep, a collective of (mostly) FSF members hell-bent on maintaining GPL and LGPL code out of tree. We would be happy to take over the maintainership burden, if you so choose. 

We would be happy to help with you as maintainer as well, and to attract more people to your project. We have a strong policy against LLM usage, but are happy with you continuing to use LLMs. 

@divyaranjan1905 please confirm.

@Pannoniae Imagine having such a weak stance on this that you resort to "the guy must use Twitter" as some sort of claimed dialectical argument.

LOL. Level 0 attack.

chardet is LGPL, period. Cope.

>  Most programmers learn from and copy short snippets out of places like StackOverflow too. 

@shcv Even if, they have judgement about whether a one-liner copy is probably generic enough and fine to take or not, the AI doesn't. You're dealing with the equivalent of a drunk coder that [doesn't know what they're doing.](https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/illusion-of-thinking) It's playing with fire, if you ask me, and feels completely unsuitable as a trustworthy base for FOSS. And the [plagiarism can be extreme](https://lcamtuf.substack.com/p/large-language-models-and-plagiarism), apparently. (Just my personal stance, not legal advice.)

PS: I literally never copy from StackOverflow if it's not just a single API call. I see how it's done, then write it myself.

Personally, I don’t think [AWOL mark](https://github.com/chardet/chardet/commit/d03506d89c7a2ef5f9fb369a75203789c251abad) should have lost the rights to this code somewhere around 2017, when someone else had been maintaining the damn thing for some 5 years.

When someone else solely maintains/controls/works on your thing for the past 10 years, it ceases to be your thing, and becomes theirs. If this was anything but open source code everyone would agree with me. Except the license nazis I suppose.

@a2mark Are you serious? Or just a troll?

@ell1e Yup, thread's a bit shorter than I remember it being....


>Anyway, how messy AI training data is seemingly continues to be occasionally ignored

Yeah, you do have a good point. IMO in the grand scheme of things it just doesn't matter. The people who care about the source of the training data aren't the ones who are using the AI. If the people who used the AI would give a damn about that, things would probably change.
